# Partnership: Sharing Profits Fairly

> When people pool money to run a business, the profit must be split
> in a way everyone accepts as fair. Partnership questions convert
> that fairness into arithmetic: multiply each partner's money by the
> time it stayed invested, and share the profit in exactly those
> ratios.

## 1. Simple Partnership: Same Time, Different Money

[definition]
A **partnership** is a business owned jointly by two or more people
(the partners), who agree in advance how profits and losses will be
shared. When all partners invest for the **same period**, the profit
is shared in the ratio of their investments.
[/definition]

**Worked example.** A, B and C invest ₹40,000, ₹50,000 and ₹60,000
in a business and earn a profit of ₹45,000 in a year.

Investment ratio $= 4 : 5 : 6$, which has $4+5+6 = 15$ parts. Each
part $= 45000/15 = 3000$. So A gets ₹12,000, B gets ₹15,000 and
C gets ₹18,000.

## 2. Compound Partnership: Different Times

When partners invest for **different periods** (or join and leave at
different dates), money alone is not fair — a rupee invested for a
year works harder than one invested for a month.

[important]
The governing rule of every partnership question:

@math label="eq:partner-rule}
\text{profit share} \propto \text{investment} \times \text{time}
@/math

Replace each partner's investment by the product
**(capital) x (months)**, and share the profit in the ratio of those
products.
[/important]

**Worked example.** A starts a business with ₹20,000. After 4 months
B joins with ₹30,000. At the year end, out of a profit of ₹27,800,
what does B receive?

A's money worked 12 months: $20000 \times 12 = 240000$.
B's money worked 8 months: $30000 \times 8 = 240000$.
The ratio is $1 : 1$, so B takes half: **₹13,900**.

## 3. Working Partners and Salary

Sometimes one partner also *works* while the others only contribute
money. A typical arrangement pays the working partner a fixed salary
(or a percentage of profit) first, and the remainder is then divided
by the investment rule.

**Worked example.** A invests ₹1,00,000 and B invests ₹50,000. B
manages the shop and is paid ₹4,000 per month from the profits. At
the year end the profit is ₹68,000. Find each partner's total
take.

Salary first: $12 \times 4000 = 48000$ leaves ₹20,000. The remaining
$20000$ is divided $100000 : 50000 = 2 : 1$ — A gets
$\frac{2}{3} \times 20000 = 13333.33$ and B gets $6666.67$. Totals:
A ≈ ₹13,333; B = 48000 + 6666.67 = **₹54,667**.

[example]
Notice the design of the answer: the money-only partner still earns
more than zero — the salary recognises labour, the ratio recognises
capital, and the exam checks that you apply them in the right
**order**: salary out first, ratio on the remainder.
[/example]

## 4. Finding Capital or Time from a Known Ratio

The rule runs backwards just as smoothly: if the profit ratio and one
capital are known, the other capital (or its time) follows.

**Worked example.** A and B share profits in the ratio $3 : 2$. B
invested ₹36,000 for 10 months. A invested ₹40,000. For how long was
A's money in the business?

@math label="eq:partner-time}
\frac{40000 \times t}{36000 \times 10} = \frac{3}{2} \;\Rightarrow\; t = \frac{3 \times 360000}{2 \times 40000} = 13.5 \text{ months}
@/math

**Worked example.** Three partners invest ₹5,000, ₹6,000 and ₹8,000
for a year. A profit of ₹7,600 is shared in what ratio, and how much
does the largest investor receive?

Investment ratio $= 5 : 6 : 8$ (19 parts); $7600/19 = 400$ per part.
Shares are ₹2,000, ₹2,400 and ₹3,200 — the largest investor takes
**₹3,200**. Well-designed questions always make the profit a clean
multiple of the part-count, so check divisibility early: if it fails,
you have misread the ratio.

## 5. Replacement, Withdrawal and Mid-Year Changes

Capital changes mid-stream are handled by splitting the year at each
change and adding the products.

**Worked example.** A invests ₹30,000. After 6 months he adds
₹10,000 more. B invests ₹50,000 throughout. Ratio of profits?

@math label="eq:partner-withdraw}
A: 30000 \times 6 + 40000 \times 6 = 420000, \qquad B: 50000 \times 12 = 600000
@/math

Ratio $= 420000 : 600000 = 7 : 10$.

| Situation | What to do |
| --- | --- |
| Partner joins late | count only the months after joining |
| Partner withdraws mid-year | split the timeline at the event, add products |
| Capital increased | same split-and-add treatment |
| Two working partners | salaries to each first, ratio on the rest |

## 6. Common Traps

- **Using the money ratio when times differ.** The single most common
  error; always multiply by time first.
- **Forgetting to pay salary/rent before ratio division** when the
  question mentions a working partner.
- **Ignoring "after 4 months"** — B's money did not work 12 months
  but 8.
- **Ratio order mix-ups.** Keep the letters in the same order on both
  sides of the colon; a flipped answer is always among the options.

## 7. Summary

Every partnership question is one sentence: **profit shares are
proportional to capital multiplied by time.** Compute each partner's
product, simplify the ratio, divide the profit by the total parts,
and distribute. Handle salaries before the ratio, split timelines at
every deposit or withdrawal, and sanity-check that shares add up to
the whole profit.
